<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:chrome="using:Avalonia.Controls.Chrome"
        xmlns:vm="using:TanMenu.Wpf.ViewModels"
        x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.MainWindow"
        Title="TanMenu"
        Width="1000" Height="688" MinWidth="711"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual"
        TransparencyLevelHint="AcrylicBlur"
        Background="Transparent"
        Icon="/wwwroot/app.ico"
        FontSize="14"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
        ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint="True"
        ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint="30">

  <!--
    The shell: an acrylic window split into a hand-drawn Windows 7 Aero caption, a CLASSIC MENU BAND
    and a page host.

    ROUND 8, TASK 3 DELETED THE NAVIGATION PANE AND PUT THE BAND WHERE IT USED TO POINT. The pane was
    a 160 DIP vertical strip down the LEFT of the client area (Round 7, Task 8 widened it from a
    48 DIP icon rail; Round 3, Task 2 had built its two-group shape). It is gone, and with it the
    ListBox that held 应用, the four command Buttons, the group separator, the five
    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRow…}" rows and the two ControlThemes that dressed them
    (Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml). What replaces it is a horizontal Menu across the top of the client
    area, below the caption — "windows 经典的 mainmenu", in the user's own words.

    FIVE ITEMS, IN THIS ORDER: 设置 · 主目录 · 刷新 · 退出 · 关于. Every one is a DIRECT COMMAND with
    no submenu, which is legal and rare rather than ordinary — PUBLISHED,
    https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/menurc/about-menus: "A menu bar typically contains
    menu names, but it can also contain command items." and "An item on the menu bar almost always
    opens a menu. Menu bars rarely contain command items." THE UX GUIDE POINTS THE OTHER WAY AND THE
    DEVIATION IS BOOKED RATHER THAN ARGUED AWAY — PUBLISHED, cmd-menus: "The presence of menus goes
    without saying in a menu bar", and cmd-toolbars sanctions this row's exact shape ("Partial
    toolbars can be combined with navigation buttons, a search box, or tabs"). A toolbar would satisfy
    every published sentence and has no published height at all, which would have dissolved the
    20-versus-23 arithmetic that forces the band to 25 DIP. The user asked for a main menu; the user's
    word wins and those sentences are the stated cost.

    刷新 IS IN THE LIST FOR A REASON THAT IS NOT SYMMETRY. LauncherViewModel.RefreshAsync is the only
    USER-REACHABLE caller of ReloadAsync(force: true) — the other is InitializeCoreAsync, the
    once-per-process startup build — and force is the only way past MenuService's fingerprint gate,
    whose own comment names a case it cannot catch: "A target exe overwritten in place under the SAME
    .lnk still needs a manual 刷新". Dropping the item would leave a manual rescan unreachable.

    THE SEARCH FIELD IS IN THE BAND, IMMEDIATELY AFTER 关于, WITH ONE SEPARATOR BETWEEN THEM AND THE
    WHOLE GROUP LEFT-ALIGNED. That is the request (放在"关于"后面) taken literally. A RIGHT-ALIGNED
    VARIANT WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED: Office XP/2003's "Type a question for help" box is the one
    adjacent Microsoft precedent, and it puts the separator a window-width away from 关于, which is a
    different layout wearing the same words. No Microsoft-shipped Windows 7 application was found with
    a text field in its menu bar at all — COULD NOT VERIFY — so this is a documented extrapolation
    rather than a reproduction, and the band's own 25 DIP says so again where it departs from the OS
    metric (SM_CYMENU MEASURED 20; see Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/Menu.axaml's header).

    THIS IS NOT THE THING ROUND 7, TASK 1 REMOVED. That was a control in the window FRAME, which
    https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/win-window-frames forbids verbatim: "Don't
    add controls to a window frame. Put the controls within the window instead." The frame is the
    caption — the Window.WindowDecorationsTheme block below. This band is inside the client area,
    under it, which is exactly where that sentence says to put controls.

    ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint pulls the whole client area — including the strip where the OS
    used to paint its own title bar — under our control. ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint is pinned to
    30, the Aero caption band's own MEASURED height (see the WindowDecorationsTheme block below for the
    capture and the coordinates), and it MUST stay equal to the height that block draws:
    WindowImpl.UpdateExtendMargins sets rect2.top = (int)(_extendTitleBarHint * RenderScaling) and
    HitTestNCA derives the OS-level caption/resize band from _extendedMargins.Top, so a mismatch produces
    a drag band that does not line up with the caption you can see. The number appears in three unlinked
    places — here, the root Grid's row 0 below, and WindowHost.TitleStripHeightDip — and
    ShellConstantTests.CaptionHeight_IsTheSameNumberInAllThreePlaces is what keeps them equal.

    THE MENU BAND'S 25 IS THE SECOND SUCH NUMBER, and it lands in three unlinked places of its own:
    the vendored Menu.axaml's <x:Double x:Key="MenuBarHeight">, WindowHost.MenuBandHeightDip (the
    fit's chrome term), and MenuBand's own Height below.
    ShellConstantTests.MenuBandHeight_IsTheSameNumberInAllThreePlaces ties all three, and it needs all
    three because two of them can agree while the third drifts — and the only symptom of that drift is
    a band whose painted height and whose fit term disagree, silently.

    There is deliberately NO MinHeight here, and MinWidth stays. Round 4, item 4: Avalonia's
    WM_GETMINMAXINFO handler sets ptMinTrackSize.Y to MinHeight × scale PLUS BorderThickness.Top+Bottom
    (decompiled Win32.WindowImpl 12.1.0), which on this window's style is the full 45+11 px frame — but
    ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint has already pulled the 45 px caption INTO the client area, so a
    declared MinHeight="568" forced a 598 DIP client, 30 DIP more than any fit ever asked for, and made
    a bottom anchor computed from the requested height overlap the taskbar. The horizontal case has no
    such error (the 22 px AdjustWindowRectEx reports IS the real horizontal inset), so MinWidth stays
    declared. The app's own 568 DIP height floor now lives in WindowHost.MinWindowHeightDip, where
    it is applied to the request instead of to the window.

    ROUND 8, TASK 3 MOVED MinWidth FROM 872 BACK TO 711 AND Width FROM 1112 BACK TO 1000, and both are
    reversions of Round 7 Task 8's pane growth rather than new numbers. The floor the four measurement
    records elsewhere in this tree actually rest on is the CONTENT PANE's 711 DIP; the pane made the
    window's own floor 711 + 161 of chrome, and with the pane deleted the chrome WIDTH is 0, so the
    window's floor IS the content pane's. WindowHost.MinWindowWidthDip mirrors the 711 and
    ShellConstantTests.MinWindowWidth_IsTheSameNumberInBothPlaces ties them;
    WindowHost.PlaceholderWidthDip mirrors the 1000 and its own remark says why the two must agree.
    THE 711 HAS ONE NEW FLOOR TO CLEAR that the old one did not: the band is five menu items, then the
    separator's whole 23 DIP footprint, then a 320 DIP field, then 11 DIP of trailing margin, laid end
    to end. Task 3 MEASURED that run rather than estimating it — see this task's report.
  -->

  <Window.Resources>

    <!-- ══ The Aero caption buttons ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         One ControlTheme for all three, differing only by width and by the ".close" class. It carries
         the app's own "Tm" prefix like every other ControlTheme this app writes, but it lives HERE
         rather than in Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml because it is window chrome, not launcher dialect,
         and because the decorations template below resolves it by {StaticResource} out of this same
         file's scope.

         GEOMETRY IS MEASURED off Microsoft's own published Windows 7 screenshot,
         https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/images/win-window-frames-image2.png
         (Calculator on Windows 7, 278x351, 24-bit RGB, native resolution — self-consistency holds on
         both axes, 228 − 8 − 8 = 212 and 321 − 30 − 8 = 283, and every border is a crisp single
         pixel): the buttons are 19 DIP tall, sit 1 DIP below the window's top edge, and measure
         28 (minimize) / 27 (maximize) / 48 (close) wide — separators at x ≈ 140 and x ≈ 168, the
         close button spanning x = 195..242, against a window whose outer right edge is x = 249.

         The FACE is AUTHORED (Tokens.axaml carries the alphas and says so). The glyphs are vector
         geometry rather than Segoe Fluent Icons code points, because the icon font is the one
         deliberate Windows 11 anachronism this conversion keeps and there is no reason to spend it on
         three shapes this simple: read off the Data strings below, minimize is ONE line, maximize is a
         closed four-sided square and close is TWO crossed lines. (This sentence said "three shapes
         that are four line segments each" until Round 7, Task 10; only maximize was ever four.) -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmCaptionButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="19" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionButtonFaceBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
          <!-- Only the BOTTOM corners are rounded: an Aero caption button is flush with the top of
               the frame and curves away from it. AUTHORED radius. It was written as RadiusControl's
               then-4 minus the 1 DIP border the face carries; Round 7, Task 7 re-valued RadiusControl
               to Aero's own 3, so the derivation no longer produces this number and the 3 is now a
               plain authored literal. It is deliberately NOT re-pointed at the token: the caption is
               window chrome measured off a Windows 7 screenshot, not a step on the app's shape
               scale, and the two happening to agree is not a reason to couple them. -->
          <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CaptionBevelLightBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="1"
                  CornerRadius="0,0,3,3">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionButtonHoverBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionButtonPressedBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- Windows 7's close button is red AT REST, not only under the pointer. -->
      <Style Selector="^.close">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionCloseFaceBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^.close:pointerover">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionCloseHoverBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:disabled">
        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

  </Window.Resources>

  <!-- ══ The Aero caption ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 7, Task 1. Avalonia 12 moved the drawn window decorations out of the Window control
       template into TopLevelHost.Decorations, driven by Window.WindowDecorationsTheme and
       Avalonia.Controls.Chrome.WindowDrawnDecorations. This block REPLACES that theme's Template
       wholesale rather than patching parts of Fluent's, and BasedOn is deliberately dropped: a full
       replacement has no use for Fluent's DefaultTitleBarHeight = 30 / DefaultFrameThickness = 1 /
       DefaultShadowThickness = 8 setters, and GetEffectiveTheme() returns the explicit theme without
       consulting the implicit {x:Type WindowDrawnDecorations} resource at all. The five Style rules
       that used to live here — suppressing PART_TitleTextPanel and PART_FullScreenButton and pulling
       PART_MinimizeButton/MaximizeButton/CloseButton flush to the top — all targeted parts of the
       template that no longer exists, so they went with it.

       WHY THE HEIGHT STILL COMES OUT AT 30 WITHOUT A DefaultTitleBarHeight SETTER: decompiled
       Avalonia 12.1.0, Window.UpdateDrawnDecorations sets decorations.TitleBarHeightOverride =
       ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint whenever that hint is >= 0, and
       WindowDrawnDecorations.UpdateEffectiveGeometry resolves TitleBarHeight from the override unless
       it is -1. The hint is 30 (top of this file), so the override wins and the default is never read.

       WHY THE UNDERLAY IS REACHABLE BY A HIT TEST, which is the whole point of drawing the caption
       here rather than in the window's own content. TopLevelHost's visual children are
       [underlay, TopLevel, overlay, popover, resizeGrips], and Win32 resolves WM_NCHITTEST through
       PresentationSource.HitTestChromeElement → RootVisual.GetVisualAt(point), where RootVisual is the
       TopLevelHost itself (decompiled: PresentationSource is constructed as
       new PresentationSource(topLevelHost, this, impl, dependencyResolver)). Nothing in the window's
       own content paints in the band — the root Grid's row 0 is an empty spacer with no Background,
       Fluent's Window template marks BOTH its PART_TransparencyFallback Border and its
       {TemplateBinding Background} Border IsHitTestVisible="False", and the one hit-testable Panel in
       that template carries Margin="{TemplateBinding WindowDecorationMargin}", which is (0,30,0,0)
       here — so the walk reaches the underlay and finds PART_TitleBar's ElementRole. The three caption
       buttons are in the OVERLAY, which sits ABOVE the window content, so they win the same walk.

       ROLES → WIN32 HIT TESTS, decompiled from Avalonia.Win32.WindowImpl.HitTestVisual: TitleBar →
       HTCAPTION (2), CloseButton → HTCLOSE (20), MinimizeButton → HTMINBUTTON (8), MaximizeButton →
       HTMAXBUTTON (9). Drag and double-click-to-maximise then come from DefWindowProc for free
       (WM_NCLBUTTONDBLCLK appears nowhere in WindowImpl), right-click on the band gives the system
       menu for free (WM_NCRBUTTONUP is not in CustomCaptionProc's switch), and
       ShouldRedirectNonClientInput returns true for exactly the three button hit-tests, which is what
       turns WM_NCMOUSEMOVE/WM_NCLBUTTONDOWN/WM_NCLBUTTONUP into RawPointerEventArgs and makes
       :pointerover, :pressed and Click work on them.

       WHAT IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE, and is therefore not attempted:
         • Aero's 8 px glass side and bottom frame. On Win32 the only decoration part ever enabled is
           TitleBar — WindowImpl.RequestedDrawnDecorations returns _isClientAreaExtended ?
           (PlatformRequestedDrawnDecoration)8 : 0 against the enum None=0, Shadow=1, Border=2,
           ResizeGrips=4, TitleBar=8 — so FrameThickness and ShadowThickness are permanently zero and
           the :has-border / :has-shadow pseudo-classes never fire. Drawing the frame anyway would mean
           drawing it INSIDE the client area, stealing 8 DIP from the content on three sides.
         • A LEFT-click system menu on the window icon. There is no HTSYSMENU member on
           WindowDecorationsElementRole, so no public role maps to it. Right-click on the band still
           raises the system menu through DefWindowProc.
         • PART_FullScreenButton, which is simply not authored. Every AttachCaptionButtons lookup is
           null-guarded (decompiled), so an absent part is legal.

       THE BUTTONS KEEP THE PART_ NAMES and nothing here wires their behaviour: AttachCaptionButtons
       finds PART_CloseButton / PART_MinimizeButton / PART_MaximizeButton in the template's name scope
       and hooks Close() / WindowState = Minimized / the maximize toggle itself, and it also sets
       AutomationProperties.AutomationId to Close / Minimize / Maximize and marks PART_TitleBar with
       AutomationId "AvaloniaTitleBar". That is where this round's UI Automation verification gets its
       identifiers from — we do not name them ourselves. -->
  <Window.WindowDecorationsTheme>
    <ControlTheme TargetType="chrome:WindowDrawnDecorations">
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <WindowDrawnDecorationsTemplate>
          <chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent>

            <!-- ══ Underlay: the glass band, its bevel, the icon and the title ══════════════════
                 The underlay renders BEHIND the window's own content. That is correct for a caption
                 the content deliberately leaves empty, and it is what makes the band impossible to
                 cover or clip from inside a page. -->
            <chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Underlay>
              <Panel>
                <Border x:Name="PART_TitleBar"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top"
                        Height="{TemplateBinding TitleBarHeight}"
                        Background="Transparent"
                        IsVisible="{TemplateBinding HasTitleBar}"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="TitleBar">
                  <Panel>

                    <!-- 1. The colour tint bar. Aero's user colour was DwmGetColorizationColor — a
                            single FRAME colour — and that is exactly the role restored here, which is
                            why this app's accent survives the conversion with a job instead of six.
                            AccentColor / AccentColorShaded are republished live by
                            Services/AppTheme.cs on every Windows personalization change, so the stops
                            are {DynamicResource} rather than a brush baked in Tokens.axaml.
                            Opacity 0.35 is AUTHORED: the accent keys are fully opaque (they also
                            drive the splash card, which needs them so), and a solid accent bar would
                            hide the acrylic the tint is supposed to sit on. -->
                    <Border Opacity="0.35">
                      <Border.Background>
                        <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
                          <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="{DynamicResource AccentColor}" />
                          <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="{DynamicResource AccentColorShaded}" />
                        </LinearGradientBrush>
                      </Border.Background>
                    </Border>

                    <!-- 2. The glass veil — the bright inner reflection Aero had and acrylic does
                            not. Three stops, brightest at 40% of the band's height; the shape is
                            measured and the alphas are authored (Tokens.axaml). CaptionGlassBrush is
                            rewritten by MainWindow.axaml.cs on every IsActive change, because
                            WindowDrawnDecorations has no active/inactive pseudo-class and no public
                            path to WindowBase.IsActive. -->
                    <Border Background="{DynamicResource CaptionGlassBrush}" />

                    <!-- 3. The 2 DIP inner bevel on the band's bottom edge, light over dark.
                            MEASURED: rows 28 and 29 of the 30 (y = 42 light, y = 43 dark in the
                            published capture, whose band is y 14..43). -->
                    <Border VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Height="1"
                            Background="{DynamicResource CaptionBevelDarkBrush}" />
                    <Border VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Height="1" Margin="0,0,0,1"
                            Background="{DynamicResource CaptionBevelLightBrush}" />

                    <!-- 4. Icon, then title. MEASURED: the title's ink starts 30 DIP from the
                            window's outer edge (glyph column histogram, first text column x ≈ 52
                            against a window whose left edge is x = 22), and the icon sits between the
                            frame and the text — so 8 + 16 + 6 = 30 reproduces it exactly. The title
                            is centred in the full 30 DIP band (measured glyph band y = 24..32, centre
                            ≈ 28, against a band centre of (14+43)/2 = 28.5).

                            FontSize 12 is Segoe UI 9 pt at 96 dpi, which
                            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-fonts gives as
                            the system font AND as the title-bar face. It is a literal rather than
                            FontSizeCaption because that token was a Windows 11 optical-size step, and
                            Round 7, Task 7 deleted it outright; the caption's size is Aero's, not the
                            ramp's. It coincides with FontSizeBody, which that same task re-valued to
                            12, and the coincidence is not made into a reference for the reason above.

                            The glow is Microsoft's own number — see CaptionTitleGlow in Tokens.axaml
                            for the DTTOPTS citation. Foreground follows the variant instead of
                            vis-fonts' literal black #000000, because black title text over a dark
                            acrylic in the dark variant would be unreadable and Windows 7 had no dark
                            variant to rule on it. -->
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <Image Width="16" Height="16" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/wwwroot/app-tile.png" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{TemplateBinding Title}"
                                 Margin="6,0,0,0"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                 FontSize="12"
                                 Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
                                 Effect="{DynamicResource CaptionTitleGlow}" />
                    </StackPanel>

                  </Panel>
                </Border>
              </Panel>
            </chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Underlay>

            <!-- ══ Overlay: the three caption buttons ═════════════════════════════════════════════
                 The overlay renders IN FRONT of the window's content, which is what a hit test has to
                 find before it finds a page. Margin "0,1,8,0" is measured: the block is top-flush
                 1 DIP down and its right edge is inset 8 DIP from the window's outer edge (block
                 x 140..242 against an outer right edge of x = 249). The 8 DIP inset is kept even
                 though no side frame is drawn, because under ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint the
                 client's right edge IS the window's outer edge — the edge the measurement is relative
                 to. 28 + 27 + 48 = 103 = the measured block width. -->
            <chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Overlay>
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                          VerticalAlignment="Top"
                          HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                          Margin="0,1,8,0"
                          IsVisible="{TemplateBinding HasTitleBar}">

                <Button x:Name="PART_MinimizeButton"
                        Theme="{StaticResource TmCaptionButton}"
                        Width="28"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MinimizeButton">
                  <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                        Data="M 0,0 L 9,0" Margin="0,6,0,0" />
                </Button>

                <Button x:Name="PART_MaximizeButton"
                        Theme="{StaticResource TmCaptionButton}"
                        Width="27"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MaximizeButton">
                  <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                        Data="M 0.5,0.5 L 8.5,0.5 L 8.5,8.5 L 0.5,8.5 Z" />
                </Button>

                <Button x:Name="PART_CloseButton"
                        Classes="close"
                        Theme="{StaticResource TmCaptionButton}"
                        Width="48"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="CloseButton">
                  <!-- White, not TextPrimaryBrush: the face under it is red in both variants. -->
                  <Path Stroke="White" StrokeThickness="1.5"
                        Data="M 0,0 L 9,9 M 9,0 L 0,9" />
                </Button>

              </StackPanel>
            </chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Overlay>

          </chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent>
        </WindowDrawnDecorationsTemplate>
      </Setter>
    </ControlTheme>
  </Window.WindowDecorationsTheme>

  <!-- Row 0 is a RESERVED SPACER and is deliberately empty. ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint pulls
       the caption band into the client area but TopLevelHost.DecorationInset stays zero on Win32
       (decompiled Window.UpdateDrawnDecorationMargins: the inset is only applied in
       _isForcedDecorationMode, i.e. when decorations are drawn WITHOUT the client area being
       extended), so the window's content is arranged over the whole window and this row is what keeps
       the menu band and the page host out from under the caption. It must carry no Background: a
       hit-testable element here would shadow the caption's own underlay in
       PresentationSource.HitTestChromeElement's topmost-first walk and the window would stop being
       draggable. The 30 is one of the three copies ShellConstantTests ties together. -->
  <Grid RowDefinitions="30,*">

    <!-- ══ THE CLIENT SURFACE, ROUND 7 TASK 7 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         This Background is what makes the app legible, and it is Aero's own structure rather than a
         compromise: in Windows 7 the GLASS was the window FRAME and the client area under it was
         opaque #FCFCFC. Row 0 above — the caption band — deliberately keeps no Background at all, so
         the acrylic still shows through it and the hand-drawn glass gradient still sits on a live
         blur. Everything below the caption is SurfaceBaseBrush, which Tokens.axaml aliases onto the
         vendored AeroWindowBackground.

         MEASURED, and this is why it is not optional. Until Task 7 the whole client was
         Transparent over the acrylic. Avalonia's AcrylicBlur on Win32 is a plain gaussian blur of
         what is behind the window with NO tint layer of its own, so every glyph on a page that
         painted nothing sat on a blur of the user's desktop. On this machine the wallpaper
         (D:\0.Documents\Pictures\壁纸, 7680x4532) averages (87,84,103) over 120,684 sampled pixels,
         whose WCAG relative luminance is 0.093. Against that surface the 设置 page's group headings
         (HeadingBrush's Light value, luminance 0.050) computed to 1.4:1 and its body ink (pure black)
         to 2.9:1 — against WCAG AA's 4.5:1 for body text. PrintWindow cannot photograph the composite
         at all (flag 2 returns (0,0,0) for a transparent client), so the failure was neither visible
         to the instrument nor arguable away: the group headings really did sample (0,0,0) ink on a
         (0,0,0) background, ratio 1.00, on the build that task replaced. Over AeroWindowBackground
         the same two elements measured 10.59:1 and 20.47:1 in Light and 4.29:1 and 11.90:1 in Dark,
         read off PrintWindow captures rather than computed. (That page is a modal dialog since
         Round 8, Task 2 and reads the same SurfaceBaseBrush from its own Background; the argument is
         about what the window's material IS, so it survives the move.)

         ══ TWO ROWS, NOT TWO COLUMNS — ROUND 8, TASK 3 ═══════════════════════════════════════════
         This Grid was ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" with the 160 DIP navigation pane in the Auto column
         and the page host in the "*" one. The pane is deleted and the chrome runs HORIZONTALLY now:
         an Auto row holding the menu band, and a "*" row holding the page. The Auto row follows
         MenuBand's own explicit Height rather than restating it, which is the same arrangement the
         deleted Auto COLUMN had with the pane's Width — the number is stated once, in the markup
         below. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" RowDefinitions="Auto,*"
          Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceBaseBrush}">

      <!-- ══ The classic menu band ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           25 DIP: the item strip is 23 and the vendored Menu template docks two 1 DIP bottom rules
           inside the same height. 23 is PUBLISHED,
           https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-layout — a one-line text box is
           23 relative pixels tall, and item 5 puts one of those in this bar. The full derivation, the
           5 DIP departure from the MEASURED SM_CYMENU of 20, and the two alternatives that were
           rejected are in Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/Menu.axaml's own header, which is also where the
           three changes made to that vendored file are recorded.

           x:DataType IS REQUIRED HERE AND IS NOT TIDINESS. AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault is
           true (TanMenu.Wpf.csproj), and a compiled {Binding} resolves its path against the nearest
           ancestor's x:DataType. The search field below moved out of Views/AppPage.axaml, which
           supplies one on its root; this Window's root does NOT and must not — the Window's own
           DataContext is not the view model, and a root-level x:DataType would mis-type every other
           binding in this file. Scoping it to the band is what makes {Binding Filter},
           {Binding SearchPlaceholderText} and {Binding ClearFilterText} compile. Without it they are
           a BUILD ERROR, not a silent no-op. The DataContext itself is assigned once, in the
           constructor: MenuBand.DataContext = _appPage.ViewModel.
           The THREE {Binding #Name.…} bindings below — MenuRunSpacer's Width and the two affordance
           faces' IsVisible — are ELEMENT-NAME bindings: they resolve against the named element's own
           type, need no x:DataType, and would have moved unchanged either way. -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="0" x:Name="MenuBand" Height="25"
            x:DataType="vm:LauncherViewModel">

        <!-- The Menu fills the whole cell, which is what makes Aero's own bar — the gradient, the
             7 DIP shine and the two bottom rules — run the FULL WINDOW WIDTH. The search group below
             is a SIBLING over it, not a child of it: MenuBase.NeedsContainerOverride accepts only
             MenuItem and Separator as their own containers (decompiled, Avalonia 12.1.0), so a
             TextBox placed in Menu.Items would be wrapped in a generated MenuItem, become its Header,
             and inherit DefaultMenuInteractionHandler's key handling and click-closes-the-menu
             behaviour. Menu also overrides KeyboardNavigation.TabNavigation to Once, so a field
             inside it might not be individually tab-reachable; a sibling is unaffected by both.

             NO ICONS AND NO ELLIPSES. PUBLISHED, cmd-menus: "If you use icons, don't feel obligated
             to provide them for all menu items", and About/Options "must display another window when
             clicked, but don't require additional information from the user. Therefore they don't
             need ellipses." Dropping the glyphs also removes five of this app's Segoe Fluent Icons
             uses, which is the one anachronism the conversion declares.

             HEADERS AND ACCESSIBLE NAMES ARE BOTH WRITTEN FROM CODE — ApplyMenuText, five Header
             writes and five AutomationProperties.Name writes, the same ten ApplyRailText did for the
             pane's rows. The Header carries the access-key marker "_" and the name does not: the
             vendored template opts in with RecognizesAccessKey="True" on PART_HeaderPresenter, and
             Avalonia's AccessText strips the marker before drawing, but a UIA name taken from the raw
             header string keeps it — MEASURED on the Options dialog's own 应用 button in Round 8,
             Task 2, which is why that button carries the same explicit pair. Access keys are
             PUBLISHED where a published assignment exists (inter-keyboard's standard table gives
             O = Options, x = Exit, A = About) and app-specific where none does (M for 主目录, on the
             guide's own preference for wide letters "such as w, m"; R for 刷新). -->
        <Menu x:Name="MainMenu">
          <MenuItem x:Name="MenuOptions" Click="OnOptionsClick" />
          <MenuItem x:Name="MenuOpenRoot" Click="OnOpenRootClick" />
          <MenuItem x:Name="MenuRefresh" Click="OnRefreshClick" />
          <MenuItem x:Name="MenuExit" Click="OnExitClick" />
          <MenuItem x:Name="MenuAbout" Click="OnAboutClick" />
        </Menu>

        <!-- ══ 关于, then the separator, then the field — LEFT-ALIGNED ═════════════════════════════
             The group starts exactly where 关于 ends, and it does that with a SPACER rather than with
             a Grid column, because the two requirements are in tension: a
             ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" with the Menu in column 0 would put the group in the
             right place and stop Aero's bar painting past the last item. So the Menu keeps the whole
             cell and the group is offset by the x of 关于's right edge, taken from the one element
             that knows it.

             MenuAbout.Bounds is stated in its items panel's coordinate space, and READ OUT OF THE
             VENDORED TEMPLATE that panel has no left inset to correct for: PART_ItemsPresenter is a
             SIBLING of the bordered Border inside the template's Panel, not its child, so the 1 DIP
             side border does not push it. Task 3 measured the gap to confirm it. MenuItem.Bounds is a
             Rect and .Right a double, so this is a plain double-to-double element-name binding with
             no converter, and it re-evaluates on every language, font and DPI change for free. There
             is no layout cycle: MenuAbout's arrange depends on the Menu, which fills the cell whatever
             this group measures.

             VerticalAlignment="Top", NOT Center, and that is a correction to the design rather than a
             preference. The band is 25 DIP and its bottom 2 belong to the template's two rules, so
             the item strip is the TOP 23 — exactly the field's own MinHeight. Centring a 23 DIP field
             in 25 DIP would push it 1 DIP down and lay its bottom edge across the inner rule, half a
             DIP out of step with the menu items beside it.

             The trailing Margin is 11 (PUBLISHED, vis-layout's dialog margin) so the field never
             touches the window's right edge at the width floor. It is a margin on this panel rather
             than a Grid column because below the floor the thing that should clip is the FIELD, not
             the menu run. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="SearchGroup" Orientation="Horizontal"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                    Margin="0,0,11,0">

          <Border x:Name="MenuRunSpacer" IsHitTestVisible="False"
                  Width="{Binding #MenuAbout.Bounds.Right}" />

          <!-- Geometry, and why this is a Border rather than a Separator, are in
               Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml's "Border.tm-bar-separator" block. -->
          <Border Classes="tm-bar-separator" />

          <!-- ══ The search field ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
               Moved here from Views/AppPage.axaml by Round 8, Task 3, with its two affordance faces,
               its four handlers and its authored 320 DIP width unchanged. THE 320 IS AUTHORED and
               nothing published fixes it: ctrl-search-boxes states no width rule at all and its
               sizing section is an image. 320 holds the longest prompt this app ships in either
               language plus the "additional 30 percent" localisation allowance ctrl-drop publishes
               (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/ctrl-drop). It is a fixed
               width, not a proportional one: the Task-Manager-measured 0.34 x client ratio that drove
               this field through Rounds 3-6 was a property of a control living in the window's own
               chrome, and ApplySearchFieldWidth died with that strip in Round 7, Task 1. Nothing here
               brings it back — a re-fit trigger is exactly what a proportional width would need.

               ctrl-search-boxes puts a primary-entry-point search box "centered at the top of the
               window" (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/ctrl-search-boxes).
               This field is at the top of the window and is NOT centred, and that is item 5's
               instruction rather than an oversight: 放在"关于"后面. Booked as a deviation, like the
               menu-bar-versus-toolbar one above. -->
          <Border Classes="tm-searchfield" x:Name="SearchField" Width="320">
            <DockPanel>
              <!-- THE TWO FACES OF THE RIGHT-END AFFORDANCE. PUBLISHED, ctrl-search-boxes
                   (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/ctrl-search-boxes): "In
                   Instant search mode, a clear button appears after the user starts typing; an 'x'
                   replaces the magnifying glass search symbol. On hover, the 'x' gets a button look
                   and can be clicked", under a figure captioned "…at the right end of the control".

                   The swap is driven off the TEXT BOX ITSELF rather than off a view-model flag, with
                   Avalonia's own StringConverters singletons — the same pair, and the same x:Static
                   form, the vendored TextBox template uses for PART_Watermark's own visibility. That
                   keeps a purely visual state out of LauncherViewModel and needs no new property to
                   raise, and it still answers the view model: SearchBox.Text is TwoWay-bound to
                   Filter, so setting Filter programmatically flips these two exactly as typing does.
                   Both are ELEMENT-NAME bindings, so they need no x:DataType and moved unchanged.

                   Both are DockPanel.Dock="Right" and both are declared BEFORE the TextBox, because a
                   DockPanel gives its LAST child the remaining space and the text box is what should
                   take it. Only one of the two is ever visible, so the field's layout does not move
                   when they swap: they are the same box with the same padding. -->
              <Border DockPanel.Dock="Right" Classes="tm-search-affordance"
                      IsVisible="{Binding #SearchBox.Text, Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNullOrEmpty}}">
                <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon subtle small" Text="&#xE721;" />
              </Border>
              <Border DockPanel.Dock="Right" x:Name="SearchClear"
                      Classes="tm-search-affordance clickable"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding ClearFilterText}"
                      IsVisible="{Binding #SearchBox.Text, Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}"
                      PointerPressed="OnSearchClearPressed">
                <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon small" Text="&#xE711;" />
              </Border>
              <!-- Background and BorderThickness are LOCAL values on purpose: a local value outranks
                   a style setter for the SAME property on the SAME element, so the TextBox itself
                   paints nothing over the fill Border.tm-searchfield already draws.

                   What that does NOT block is a theme rule aimed at a NAMED PART inside the TextBox
                   control template — a different element, with no local value of its own, whatever
                   is set here. Under the vendored Aero theme that part is Border#BgElement and the
                   rules that reach it are :pointerover and :focus. Only the :pointerover one is
                   suppressed: see Launcher.axaml's
                   "TextBox.tm-search:pointerover /template/ Border#BgElement" rule for why, why the
                   BorderBrush half needs no rule, and why Round 8, Task 7 measured the :focus twin
                   inert and deleted it. -->
              <TextBox x:Name="SearchBox"
                       Classes="tm-search"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding SearchPlaceholderText}"
                       Background="Transparent"
                       BorderThickness="0"
                       PlaceholderText="{Binding SearchPlaceholderText}"
                       Text="{Binding Filter, Mode=TwoWay}"
                       KeyDown="OnSearchKeyDown"
                       GotFocus="OnSearchFocusChanged"
                       LostFocus="OnSearchFocusChanged" />
            </DockPanel>
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>

      <!-- ══ Content pane: the opaque 应用 surface ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
           The acrylic reaches ONE band and one only — row 0, the caption. Everything below it is the
           opaque SurfaceBaseBrush the content Grid above paints, so "the window's own material" below
           this point means AeroWindowBackground, not a backdrop. On the 应用 page that surface is
           covered again, by this Border. The page paints no surface of its own — AppPage's root Panel
           sets only Transparent — which is what lets this single Border decide the surface under it.

           Round 4, item 5 gave the shell's two pages two DIFFERENT looks on this one Border, and
           Round 6, item 2 flipped which of them was the exception. ROUND 8, TASK 2 ENDED THE PAIR by
           ending the plurality — 设置 became a modal dialog, so the :not(.app) rule lost its only
           subject — and ROUND 8, TASK 3 ENDED THE CLASS: there is one rule now, Border.tm-page-pane,
           and the markup states no "app" any more.

           THE 1 DIP TOP+LEFT HAIRLINE IS GONE WITH IT, and that is this task's one visible change to
           this element. Its LEFT edge separated the page from the navigation pane, which no longer
           exists; its TOP edge is redundant against the two 1 DIP bottom rules the menu band's own
           vendored template draws directly above it. Both DIP came out of the fit in the same commit
           — MainWindow.PaneBorderDip is deleted, the chrome WIDTH term is 0, and
           WindowHost.ChromeHeightDip is TitleStripHeightDip + MenuBandHeightDip with no "+1" left.

           ROUND 7, TASK 7 CHANGED WHAT THE LOOK IS MADE OF, and that half is unchanged here. The fill
           is SurfacePageBrush aliased onto the vendored AeroListBoxBackground — Explorer's own content
           area was a list background — and the 8 DIP top-left arc is GONE: Aero's ListBox sets
           CornerRadius="0" and a Windows 7 content pane has no rounded corner, so RadiusPaneTopLeft
           was deleted with its measurement. The backdrop-refused fallback is unchanged and still the
           single source of truth for the window's colour (Window.Background, set by ApplyBackdrop);
           this Border must never grow a fallback of its own. Why the look is a STYLE rather than local
           attributes here is in Launcher.axaml's "Border.tm-page-pane" block — a style setter cannot
           override a local value in Avalonia, in either direction.

           ClipToBounds — AND WHY THE EVIDENCE THAT ONCE JUSTIFIED IT IS NOW MOOT TWICE OVER. Round 4
           recorded here that a Border clips its child to its CORNER RADIUS rather than to its bounds
           rectangle, on one observation: the 关于 overlay's square accent card traced an arc down its
           first eleven rows on a PrintWindow capture. Round 5, item 2 deleted that overlay, so the
           observation lost its subject; Round 7, Task 7 deleted the arc, so it has lost its mechanism
           as well — this pane's corners are square and a radius clip and a bounds clip are the same
           rectangle. ClipToBounds stays because it costs nothing and because a full-bleed child of
           AppPage's OverlayPanel (whose ArrangeOverride arranges every child over the WHOLE panel
           rect) would otherwise be free to paint outside the pane. None of the five children it has
           today can: the DockPanel's own children inset themselves, and SplashPanel, both tm-empty
           panels and tm-toast are all centred. -->
      <Border Grid.Row="1"
              x:Name="PagePane"
              Classes="tm-page-pane"
              ClipToBounds="True">
        <ContentControl x:Name="PageHost" />
      </Border>
    </Grid>
  </Grid>
</Window>
